# Jekyll build output and caches — never committed.
_site/
.jekyll-cache/
.jekyll-metadata
.sass-cache/

# Bundler
vendor/
.bundle/

# 📌 Gemfile.lock IS DELIBERATELY NOT COMMITTED, and that is a decision
# rather than an oversight.
#
# The usual argument for committing it is reproducible builds. That is worth
# a lot on an app with fifty gems; it is worth almost nothing here, because
# the Gemfile has ONE real dependency and it is already pinned to a major
# (`jekyll "~> 4.4"`), so nothing can move further than a Jekyll 4 minor.
#
# What a committed lock costs is the commoner failure: it records a PLATFORMS
# list and a `BUNDLED WITH` version, and both have to agree with the machine
# running `bundle install`. A lock written on one Ruby, one Bundler and one
# architecture is the single most frequent reason a Ruby build that worked
# last month fails today — on a different Mac, on a newer Ruby, or on a CI
# runner whose Bundler normalises a platform string differently.
#
# Weighing the two: a Jekyll minor changing how a page renders is unlikely,
# visible at once, and fixed by tightening one line. A lock that will not
# install is likely, cryptic, and blocks publishing. So the lock stays out.
#
# ↩️ TO REVERSE IT, if you ever want the versions frozen exactly:
#     cd site
#     bundle lock --add-platform ruby arm64-darwin x86_64-linux
#     git add -f Gemfile.lock
# Naming all three platforms is what stops the lock working on one machine
# and refusing on another.
Gemfile.lock
